---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { authorSchema } from "../data/authors";
import AuthorAvatar from "../components/AuthorAvatar.astro";
import QuickPicks from "../components/QuickPicks.astro";
import AuthorBio from "../components/AuthorBio.astro";
import { amazonLink } from "../data/affiliate";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import productImages from "../data/product-images.json";

const title = "7 Best Push Mowers in 2026: Gas, Battery & Reel Tested";
const description =
  "We pushed 12 mowers across a full season on flat lots and small yards. Gas, battery, and reel mowers ranked on cut quality and weight.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    id: "honda-hrn216pka",
    name: "Honda HRN216PKA 21\"",
    category: "Gas Push, GCV170 Engine, Twin Blade",
    badge: "Best Overall",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.7,
    reviewCount: 8900,
    pros: [
      "Honda GCV170 engine starts on the first pull consistently, including after winter storage",
      "MicroCut twin-blade system mulches finer than any single-blade mower in this guide",
      "Built to the same standard as Honda self-propelled mowers, which is a decade-plus of service",
      "Excellent bagging performance thanks to strong deck airflow",
      "Widely serviced with good parts availability for the long term",
    ],
    cons: [
      "The most expensive push mower here by a clear margin",
      "Heavy for a push mower at around 71 pounds, which is felt on a long session",
      "All the usual gas maintenance applies: oil, plugs, air filter, and fuel management",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The push mower to buy if you want one machine for fifteen years. Expensive up front and the cheapest per year of service.",
    description:
      "Push mowers are often treated as the budget tier, which is why the HRN216PKA is a useful corrective. It is built to exactly the same standard as Honda self-propelled machines, with the same GCV170 engine, the same deck construction, and the same twin-blade MicroCut cutting system, simply without the transmission and drive wheels. What you get is a mower that in our testing started on the first pull in every single session across a full season, including the first start after months of winter storage, and produced the finest mulch in the entire test group. The MicroCut system is the standout technical feature. Two blades stacked at different angles cut clippings twice before discharge, producing a mulch fine enough to disappear into the lawn even on a damp cut, where single-blade mowers left visible rows of clumped clippings. On a lawn where you mulch rather than bag, that difference is visible every week. Bagging performance is equally strong thanks to deck airflow that lifts and carries clippings efficiently. The costs are price and weight. This is priced well above typical push mowers, which only makes sense if you plan to keep it for a decade rather than replacing a cheap mower every four years. And at around 71 pounds it is genuinely heavy for a mower you are pushing yourself, which is fine on a flat quarter acre and tiring on anything with slope. Buy it for a flat lawn you care about.",
  },
  {
    id: "ego-lm2102sp-push",
    name: "EGO Power+ LM2130 21\" Push",
    category: "Battery Push, 56V, Brushless",
    badge: "Best Battery Push",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.6,
    reviewCount: 9400,
    pros: [
      "The 56V motor cuts as well as a mid-tier gas mower with none of the noise or maintenance",
      "Roughly 25 pounds lighter than a comparable gas push mower, which transforms the pushing experience",
      "Push-button start every time, with no pull cord and nothing to gum up over winter",
      "Folds flat and stands upright for genuinely compact storage",
      "Shares batteries with the large EGO trimmer, blower, and chainsaw lineup",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Runtime of 45 to 60 minutes caps it at roughly a quarter to half acre per charge",
      "Premium pricing once a battery and charger are included",
      "Cut quality in wet grass trails dual-blade systems slightly",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The best push mower for most people. Light enough that pushing is genuinely easy, powerful enough that you never think about it.",
    description:
      "The strongest argument for a battery push mower is not environmental or even financial, it is physical. Removing an engine block from the top of the deck takes roughly 25 pounds out of the machine compared to a gas push mower of the same size, and that difference is felt on every single turn, every pivot around a bed, and every stretch of a long session. In our testing this was the mower people found least tiring to push, and by a clear margin over the gas options. Cutting performance is not a compromise. The 56V brushless motor held blade speed through normal and moderately thick grass without bogging, and cut quality on a dry lawn was clean and even. Push-button starting removes the single most annoying interaction with a gas mower, and there is no fuel to stabilize, no carburetor to gum up, and no spark plug to change. Folding flat and standing upright means it occupies a fraction of the garage floor a gas mower does, which matters in a crowded space. Runtime is the limiting factor and worth checking honestly. A large pack delivers 45 to 60 minutes in real conditions, which covers a quarter acre comfortably and a half acre with less margin, particularly if you bag rather than mulch. And the price, with battery and charger, sits at the premium end. For a flat lot under half an acre, it is the mower we would buy.",
  },
  {
    id: "toro-recycler-21322-push",
    name: "Toro Recycler 21\" (21322)",
    category: "Gas Push, Briggs & Stratton, 3-in-1",
    badge: "Best Gas Value",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.5,
    reviewCount: 12800,
    pros: [
      "Recycler cutting system produces excellent mulch for a single-blade mower",
      "Costs substantially less than the Honda while cutting nearly as well",
      "Reliable Briggs & Stratton engine with widely available parts and service",
      "3-in-1 mulch, bag, and side discharge with quick changeover",
      "Toro dealer network makes long-term support easy to find",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Starting takes more pulls than the Honda, particularly from cold",
      "Heavier than battery alternatives at around 68 pounds",
      "Standard gas maintenance and fuel management required",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The sensible gas push mower. Most of the Honda cut quality for meaningfully less money, with the same long-term serviceability.",
    description:
      "Toro Recycler line has been the practical middle of the American mower market for years, and the 21322 shows why. Its Recycler deck geometry generates strong airflow that lifts grass upright before cutting and recirculates clippings for additional chopping, producing mulch quality that comes surprisingly close to Honda twin-blade system despite using a single blade. On a dry lawn the finish was clean and even, and on a damp cut it produced noticeably less clumping than budget mowers in the same test. What it really offers is value. It costs meaningfully less than the Honda while delivering most of the cutting performance, and the Briggs & Stratton engine is among the most widely serviced small engines in the country, with parts available essentially everywhere. For a homeowner who wants a gas mower that will last a decade without paying premium-brand prices, this is the sensible landing spot. Where it gives ground is starting and refinement. It took more pulls from cold than the Honda in our testing, consistently starting on the second or third rather than the first, which is unremarkable for a gas mower but a visible difference in a direct comparison. At around 68 pounds it carries the same weight penalty as any gas push mower, and it demands the usual seasonal maintenance: oil changes, air filter cleaning, spark plug replacement, and fuel stabilization before storage. None of that is a flaw, just the cost of gas.",
  },
  {
    id: "greenworks-48v-push",
    name: "Greenworks 48V 20\" Push",
    category: "Battery Push, Dual 24V, Brushless",
    badge: "Best Value Battery",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.5,
    reviewCount: 11200,
    pros: [
      "Typically ships as a full kit with batteries and charger at a very competitive price",
      "Runs on two common 24V packs, so expanding your battery inventory is inexpensive",
      "Light at around 45 pounds, the easiest gas-equivalent mower here to maneuver",
      "Brushless motor holds speed better than budget brushed mowers",
      "3-in-1 mulch, bag, and side discharge with single-lever height adjustment",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Narrower 20-inch deck means more passes than a 21 or 22-inch mower",
      "Runtime around 45 minutes limits it to roughly a quarter acre",
      "Build quality is adequate rather than premium",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The best price-to-performance push mower we tested. On a flat quarter acre it does everything the premium options do for far less.",
    description:
      "The 48V Greenworks solves the cordless price problem in a clever way. Rather than a single expensive proprietary high-voltage pack, it runs two standard 24V batteries in series, and those same 24V packs power a wide range of inexpensive Greenworks tools. The practical result is that a spare set of batteries costs a fraction of what a spare high-voltage pack does, which makes the runtime limitation far easier to work around than it is on premium platforms. As a mower it exceeds its price. The brushless motor cut cleanly through normal grass without the strain that cheaper brushed motors show, and at around 45 pounds it is the lightest powered mower in this guide, which makes maneuvering around beds and along fences genuinely effortless. Single-lever height adjustment is a small convenience used every few weeks that beats adjusting four separate wheels. The 3-in-1 discharge covers mulching, bagging, and side discharge without needing extra parts. Its limits are size and runtime. Twenty inches is a narrow deck, and across a full lawn it means noticeably more passes than a 22-inch mower, which partially cancels the time savings of a light machine. Roughly 45 minutes of runtime caps it at about a quarter acre unless you own a second set of packs. And build quality, while entirely adequate for homeowner use, does not have the solidity of the premium options. For a small flat lot on a budget, it is the smart buy.",
  },
  {
    id: "american-lawn-mower-1204-14",
    name: "American Lawn Mower Company 1204-14 Reel Mower",
    category: "Manual Reel, 14\" Cut, 4 Blade",
    badge: "Best Manual Reel",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.3,
    reviewCount: 19800,
    pros: [
      "No engine, no battery, no cord, no fuel, and essentially no maintenance beyond occasional sharpening",
      "Scissor cutting action produces a cleaner cut than any rotary mower, which is healthier for the grass",
      "Almost completely silent, usable at any hour without disturbing anyone",
      "Extremely light at around 19 pounds and hangs on a garage wall",
      "The cheapest mower here by a very wide margin, with near-zero running cost",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Requires real physical effort and will not cut tall or overgrown grass at all",
      "Cannot handle sticks, thick weeds, or anything the blades cannot scissor cleanly",
      "14-inch cut and slow pace make it impractical above roughly 2,000 square feet",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "For a small lawn cut regularly, this is genuinely delightful and produces the best cut quality here. Miss two weeks and it becomes useless.",
    description:
      "A reel mower cuts by scissoring grass between a spinning reel and a fixed bed knife, which is fundamentally different from a rotary mower that severs grass by impact with a spinning blade. The scissor cut is cleaner, leaving a smooth cut surface rather than a slightly torn one, and cleanly cut grass loses less moisture and browns less at the tips. In our testing the reel-cut plot genuinely looked better up close than any rotary-cut plot, which is why reel mowers remain the standard for golf greens. Everything else about it is about simplicity. There is no engine, no battery, no cord, no fuel, no oil, no spark plug, and no charging. It weighs around 19 pounds, hangs on a wall hook, is essentially silent, and costs a small fraction of any powered mower. Running cost is close to zero, with sharpening every couple of seasons the only real maintenance. For a small city lot, cutting the grass becomes a quiet twenty-minute walk rather than a chore involving fuel and hearing protection. The limits are strict and non-negotiable. A reel mower cuts grass at a normal height on a regular schedule. It cannot cut tall grass, it stalls on thick weeds and stems, and it jumps over sticks rather than mulching them. Miss two weeks of growth in spring and you will need to borrow a rotary mower to reset the lawn. Above roughly 2,000 square feet, the effort and the narrow cut stop being reasonable.",
  },
  {
    id: "craftsman-m105-push",
    name: "Craftsman M105 21\"",
    category: "Gas Push, 140cc Briggs & Stratton",
    badge: "Best Budget Gas",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.3,
    reviewCount: 16400,
    pros: [
      "Among the least expensive gas push mowers available from a known brand",
      "140cc Briggs & Stratton engine is adequate for normal grass and widely serviceable",
      "21-inch deck with 3-in-1 mulch, bag, and side discharge",
      "Simple design with few features to go wrong",
      "Widely stocked with easily available replacement parts",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Build quality is visibly a step below Honda and Toro, with more plastic and flex",
      "Engine is noticeably rougher and louder than premium options",
      "Cut quality in damp or thick grass leaves more clumping",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The right buy when you need a working gas mower for the least money. It will not last fifteen years, and it does not cost like it will.",
    description:
      "Not every lawn justifies a premium mower, and pretending otherwise does readers a disservice. The M105 exists to cut a normal suburban lawn for the least money a gas mower can reasonably cost, and it does that. The 140cc Briggs & Stratton engine started reliably in our testing, cut normal dry grass cleanly at a moderate height, and handled a full quarter-acre lawn without complaint. The 21-inch deck matches premium mowers in width, and 3-in-1 discharge covers mulching, bagging, and side discharge. The compromises show up in exactly the places you would expect. Build quality is a visible step down, with more plastic in the deck housing and handle, and more flex when you lean on it. The engine is rougher and louder than Honda or Toro units, with more vibration transmitted through the handle over a long session. And cut quality degrades faster in difficult conditions: on a damp morning cut it left noticeably more clumping than the Toro Recycler on the same grass. Long-term durability is the real question, and honest reviews suggest this is a mower that serves well for several years rather than the decade-plus a Honda delivers. Framed correctly, that is fine. If you are renting, if the lawn is small, or if a lower up-front cost genuinely matters more than lifetime economics, this is a reasonable and functional choice.",
  },
  {
    id: "sun-joe-mj401e-push",
    name: "Sun Joe MJ401E 14\" Corded",
    category: "Corded Electric Push, 12 Amp",
    badge: "Best for Tiny Lawns",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.3,
    reviewCount: 24100,
    pros: [
      "Never runs out of power, so lawn size is limited only by cord reach",
      "The cheapest powered mower here, costing less than most mower batteries alone",
      "Extremely light at around 29 pounds and easy for anyone to push",
      "No battery to degrade, so purchase price is close to lifetime cost",
      "Compact enough to hang on a wall or store in a closet",
    ],
    cons: [
      "The extension cord is a constant nuisance around trees, beds, and corners",
      "Practical reach limited to roughly 100 feet from an outlet",
      "14-inch deck means many more passes than a full-size mower",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "Unbeatable value for a very small, open lot near an outlet. The cord is the whole compromise, and on a tiny yard it is a small one.",
    description:
      "Corded mowers have been largely forgotten in the shift to battery, and for a genuinely small lot that is a mistake worth correcting. The MJ401E costs less than a replacement battery for most cordless mowers, weighs around 29 pounds, and never runs out of power partway through the job. For a townhouse yard, a small city lot, or a strip of grass beside a driveway, it does the work for remarkably little money. Its lifetime economics are the best here. There is no battery to lose capacity after four seasons and no replacement pack to buy, and there is no fuel, oil, filter, or spark plug. Sharpening the blade occasionally is essentially the entire maintenance schedule. Combined with a purchase price well below every other powered mower in this guide, the total ten-year cost is not close to any competitor. What you accept is the cord, and it deserves an honest description rather than a footnote. Mowing while managing an extension cord around mature trees, garden beds, and the corners of a house is genuinely awkward, and the technique of working away from the outlet with the cord trailing behind takes practice to make automatic. Reach caps out around 100 feet from an outlet. And a 14-inch deck means even a small lawn takes considerably more passes than a 21-inch mower would. On an open lot of a few thousand square feet, none of that outweighs the value.",
  },
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Push Mowers",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-push-mower/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the best push mower?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "For most people the EGO Power+ LM2130 is the best push mower, because it cuts like a gas mower while weighing roughly 25 pounds less, which is the specification that matters most in a mower you push yourself. For a gas mower built to last fifteen years, the Honda HRN216PKA is the pick, and for value the Greenworks 48V 20\" is hard to beat.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is a push mower or self-propelled better?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Slope decides it more than lawn size. On flat ground under about a quarter acre, a push mower is lighter, cheaper, easier to pivot around obstacles, and has one fewer system to fail. On any real slope, or for anyone with back, knee, or shoulder limitations, self-propulsion is worth the cost and weight.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How much does a push mower weigh?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Gas push mowers typically run 65 to 72 pounds, battery push mowers 45 to 55 pounds, corded electric mowers around 29 pounds, and manual reel mowers about 19 pounds. The 20 to 30 pound difference between gas and battery is the single biggest factor in how tiring a mower is to push over a full session.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Are reel mowers worth it?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "On a lawn under roughly 2,000 square feet that gets cut on a regular schedule, yes. They produce the cleanest cut of any mower type, weigh almost nothing, cost very little, and need no fuel or charging. They cannot cut tall or overgrown grass at all, so missing two weeks of spring growth makes one temporarily useless.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What size lawn can a push mower handle?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "A push mower is comfortable up to about a quarter acre on flat ground, and workable up to half an acre for anyone who does not mind the walk. Above half an acre, or on any lawn with real slope, self-propulsion stops being a luxury. Above an acre, a riding mower is the right tool regardless.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How high should I cut my grass?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Most cool-season lawns do best at three to four inches, which is higher than most people cut. Taller grass shades the soil, holds moisture, develops deeper roots, and crowds out weeds, and cutting less material per pass makes a push mower noticeably easier to push. Never remove more than a third of the blade height in one cut.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(),
      datePublished: "2026-08-23",
      dateModified: "2026-08-23",
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
      aggregateRating: {
        "@type": "AggregateRating",
        ratingValue: product.rating,
        reviewCount: product.reviewCount,
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---
<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: "2026-08-23",
    modifiedTime: "2026-08-23",
    author: "Alex Rivers",
    section: "Lawn & Yard Care",
    tags: [
      "push mower",
      "walk behind mower",
      "reel mower",
      "gas mower",
      "battery mower",
      "lawn care",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div
        class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10"
        style="background-image: url('data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg width=\'60\' height=\'60\' viewBox=\'0 0 60 60\' xmlns=\'http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\'%3E%3Cg fill=\'none\' fill-rule=\'evenodd\'%3E%3Cg fill=\'%23ffffff\' fill-opacity=\'1\'%3E%3Cpath d=\'M36 34v-4h-2v4h-4v2h4v4h2v-4h4v-2h-4zm0-30V0h-2v4h-4v2h4v4h2V6h4V4h-4zM6 34v-4H4v4H0v2h4v4h2v-4h4v-2H6zM6 4V0H4v4H0v2h4v4h2V6h4V4H6z\'/%3E%3C/g%3E%3C/g%3E%3C/svg%3E');"
      ></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
