          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Do You Actually Need Self-Propelled?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Self-propulsion is the single most common upsell in mower shopping, and it is genuinely worth the money on the right property. On the wrong one it is added cost, added weight, and an extra system that eventually needs a drive belt or cable replaced. Here is how to tell which you have.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Slope is the deciding factor, not size.</strong> On flat ground, pushing a 50-pound battery mower across a quarter acre is a mild walk. On a 15-degree slope, pushing a 70-pound gas mower uphill is genuine physical work, and that is where a drive system earns its cost. Walk the steepest part of your lawn. If you naturally lean forward or slow down to keep footing, self-propulsion is worth it.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Weight changes the calculation.</strong> A battery push mower typically weighs 20 to 30 pounds less than a gas mower of the same deck size because there is no engine block on the deck. Many people who assume they need self-propulsion because their old gas mower was exhausting find that a lighter battery push mower is entirely comfortable on the same lawn.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Obstacles favor push mowers.</strong> A lawn full of beds, trees, and tight corners requires constant pivoting, and a push mower is genuinely easier to turn and reposition than a self-propelled machine whose drive wheels resist being redirected. On a complex small lot, push is often the more pleasant tool.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Physical considerations override everything.</strong> If back, knee, or shoulder issues make pushing difficult, buy self-propelled regardless of lawn size. This guide is about what a push mower does well, not about talking anyone out of a tool that keeps mowing sustainable for them.
          </p>

