            Push mowers come in four genuinely different forms, and unlike self-propelled mowers where gas and battery dominate, all four are viable choices depending on the lawn.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Gas Push</h3>

          <p>
            Unlimited runtime and the most power for thick or tall grass. Heavier than every alternative, requires seasonal maintenance and fuel management, and is loud enough to want hearing protection. Best for larger flat lawns, for grass that regularly gets ahead of you, and for anyone who does not want to think about runtime.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Battery Push</h3>

          <p>
            The sweet spot for most modern suburban lots. Substantially lighter than gas, instant starting, near-silent, and essentially maintenance-free apart from the blade. Runtime of 45 to 60 minutes caps the practical lawn size at roughly a quarter to half acre unless you own a spare pack.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Corded Electric</h3>

          <p>
            The cheapest powered option with the lowest lifetime cost, since there is no battery to replace. Never runs out of power. The cord is a genuine practical limitation around obstacles and caps usable range at about 100 feet from an outlet. Excellent for small open lots.
          </p>

