        <div id="deck-and-cut" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Deck Width, Cut Quality, and What Actually Shows
          </h2>

          <p>
            Two mowers cutting the same lawn to the same height can produce visibly different results, and the reasons are mostly in the deck rather than the engine.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Deck Width</h3>

          <p>
            A wider deck covers more ground per pass and reduces total mowing time, which is why 21 and 22-inch mowers dominate the market. But a wider deck is also heavier, harder to maneuver in tight spaces, and harder to store. On a lot with lots of beds and obstacles, a 20-inch deck often finishes faster in practice than a 22-inch one, because less time goes into repositioning.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Deck Airflow</h3>

          <p>
            A well-designed deck creates a vacuum that lifts grass blades upright immediately before they are cut, producing a more even height across the deck width and better mulching. This is the single biggest reason premium mowers cut better than budget ones at the same blade speed, and it is why decks are shaped and baffled rather than being simple flat pans.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Single Blade vs Twin Blade</h3>

          <p>
            Twin-blade systems cut clippings a second time before discharge, producing a much finer mulch that settles into the turf instead of sitting on top. The difference is most visible on a damp cut, where single-blade mowers leave rows of wet clumps and twin-blade mowers do not. Honda MicroCut is the best implementation in this guide.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Blade Sharpness Beats Everything</h3>

          <p>
            A dull blade tears grass instead of slicing it, leaving frayed tips that brown within a day and give the whole lawn a dull cast. Sharpening once or twice a season costs nothing and improves cut quality more than upgrading from a budget mower to a premium one would. This is the most neglected variable in lawn appearance.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-sm font-black text-amber-800 mb-2">The One-Third Rule</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-amber-700">
              Never remove more than a third of the grass blade height in a single cut. Taking more shocks the plant, forces it to divert energy from root growth to leaf regrowth, and leaves the lawn stressed and prone to weeds. If grass has gotten away from you, cut high, wait three or four days, and cut again rather than scalping it back in one pass.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 5: Are Reel Mowers Actually Practical? -->
        <div id="reel-mowers" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Are Reel Mowers Actually Practical?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Manual reel mowers occupy a strange position: either the best mower someone has ever owned or a purchase abandoned after two weeks, with very little middle ground. The difference is almost entirely about lawn size and mowing discipline.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>The cut is genuinely better.</strong> A reel mower scissors grass between a spinning reel and a fixed bed knife, producing a clean cut surface rather than the slightly torn one a rotary blade leaves. Cleanly cut grass loses less moisture, browns less at the tips, and recovers faster. This is why golf greens are cut with reel mowers rather than rotary ones.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>The effort is real but often overstated.</strong> On a lawn cut on a regular schedule at a normal height, a modern reel mower rolls easily and cutting a small lot takes about as long as it would with a powered mower. Most of the reputation for difficulty comes from people using them on grass that has already gotten too tall.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>The failure mode is missing a cut.</strong> A reel mower simply cannot cut tall grass. The reel pushes long blades forward rather than scissoring them, and the mower rides over the top. Two weeks of spring growth is enough to make it useless until the lawn is reset with a rotary mower. If your schedule is unpredictable, this is a serious limitation.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Weeds and debris stop it.</strong> Thick weed stems, dandelion stalks, and any twig thicker than a pencil will jam or bounce over rather than cut. A lawn with significant weed pressure is not a good candidate until the weeds are dealt with separately.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>The size ceiling is real.</strong> With a 14 to 16-inch cut and a walking pace, anything above roughly 2,000 square feet becomes a genuinely long job. Within that limit, it is quiet, cheap, requires no fuel or charging, and hangs on a garage wall taking almost no space.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 6: Mowing Technique That Makes a Push Mower Easier -->
        <div id="technique" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Mowing Technique That Makes a Push Mower Easier
          </h2>

          <p>
            A push mower rewards technique in a way self-propelled machines do not, because every inefficiency costs you physical effort directly.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Raise the cutting height.</strong> Taller grass shades the soil, holds moisture, develops deeper roots, and crowds out weeds, and cutting less material per pass means noticeably less resistance to push. Most cool-season lawns do best at three to four inches, which is higher than most people cut and easier work in every respect.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Mulch instead of bagging where you can.</strong> A bag full of wet clippings adds real weight to a mower you are pushing, and it fills fastest exactly when the grass is heaviest. Mulching removes that weight entirely, returns nitrogen to the lawn, and eliminates the stops to empty the bag.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Cut when the grass is dry.</strong> Wet grass is heavier, clumps in the deck, sticks to the underside, and increases push resistance substantially. A lawn cut in the late afternoon after dew has burned off takes measurably less effort than the same lawn cut at eight in the morning.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Plan your pattern to minimize turns.</strong> Long straight passes with a turn at each end are far less work than a pattern requiring frequent direction changes, because pivoting a mower is where most of the effort goes on a lot with obstacles. Mow the perimeter first, then run long parallel passes inside it.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Keep the deck clean.</strong> Packed grass on the underside of the deck restricts the airflow that lifts grass for cutting, which forces the motor to work harder and increases how much you have to push. Scraping the deck out every few mows during heavy growth genuinely reduces effort.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 7: Push Mower Maintenance by Power Type -->
        <div id="maintenance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Push Mower Maintenance by Power Type
          </h2>

          <p>
            Push mowers have one fewer system to maintain than self-propelled machines, since there is no drive belt, cable, or transmission. What remains is straightforward.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>All types: sharpen the blade.</strong> Once or twice per season, or immediately after hitting anything solid. Remove the blade with a socket wrench, wedging a block of wood against it to stop rotation, then sharpen with a file or bench grinder maintaining the original angle. Balance it afterward on a nail through the center hole so it does not vibrate.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Gas mowers: fuel is the main enemy.</strong> Ethanol-blended gasoline degrades within weeks and gums up small carburetors, which is the single most common reason a mower will not start in spring. Either run the tank dry before storage or add a stabilizer. Change the oil annually, clean or replace the air filter each season, and replace the spark plug every year or two.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Battery mowers: care for the pack.</strong> Store at roughly half charge in a temperate place rather than fully charged in a hot garage. Bring packs indoors for winter. Do not spray water into the deck when cleaning, since motors and control boards live in places gas mowers do not put them.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Corded mowers: inspect the cord.</strong> Check for nicks and cuts in the insulation before each use, and never mow over the cord, which is easier to do than it sounds. Use an outdoor-rated extension cord of adequate gauge for the length, since an undersized cord causes voltage drop that makes the motor work harder and run hot.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Reel mowers: adjust the bed knife.</strong> The reel should just barely contact the bed knife, close enough to scissor cleanly without dragging. Most models adjust with a pair of screws, and the classic test is whether the mower cuts a sheet of paper held between reel and knife. Sharpening kits are inexpensive and needed only every year or two.
          </p>
        </div>

