        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Push Mower Mistakes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Push mowers are simple machines, and the mistakes people make with them are correspondingly simple to avoid.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a Heavy Gas Mower for a Small Flat Lot</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 70-pound gas push mower on a quarter-acre flat lawn is significantly more work than a 48-pound battery mower doing the same job, with no cutting advantage at that size. People often conclude they need self-propulsion when what they actually needed was a lighter machine.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Cutting Too Short</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Scalping the lawn to stretch the interval between mows backfires completely. Short grass has shallow roots, dries out faster, and lets weed seeds reach sunlight and germinate. Cutting at three to four inches produces a healthier lawn and is easier work, because you are moving less material with every pass.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Bagging Everything Out of Habit</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Bagging adds real weight to a mower you are pushing, fills fastest exactly when grass is heaviest, and throws away nitrogen the lawn could reuse. Unless the grass is very tall or you are dealing with disease, mulching produces a better lawn with less effort and no trips to empty a bag.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Storing a Gas Mower With Untreated Fuel</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Ethanol-blended gasoline degrades in weeks and gums up carburetors, which is the leading reason mowers will not start in spring. Run the tank dry or add stabilizer before storage, and consider ethanol-free fuel for seasonal equipment. Ten minutes in the fall prevents a repair bill in April.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

